# consumer proof: beyond PR #166 (issues #151-#165, taken past the easy win)

Host: Linux x86_64, uid 0, 2026-10-01. /workspace exec (20GB free, chosen),
/tmp + /dev/shm + /state noexec here (space --probe says noexec for all
three), so the plan picks the roomy exec-capable root. memfd_create blocked
by seccomp, views fall back to copies, same as the last round.
ISO: HOME=/workspace/.sandhome-proof/home, exec=/workspace/.sandhome-proof/exec.

## setup

sh bootstrap.sh --toolset developer --exec /workspace/.sandhome-proof/exec
  8.6s, failures=0. doctor_failures=0 with cache_dir_exec, tar_no_same_owner,
  node_npm_js live.
sandhome install deno meson rust -> doctor_failures=0
  (deno_spawn, rustup_writable, node_npm_js all yes)

## builds that run

- cargo new hello; cargo build (CARGO_TARGET_DIR defaulted to
  $SANDHOME_EXEC/target-workspace); artifact runs. (beyond #156)
- rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown + wasm32-unknown-emscripten,
  both rc=0 and listed installed. (beyond #159)
- emcc hello.c -o hello.js; node hello.js prints wasm-ok. (beyond #163)
- cargo build --target wasm32-unknown-emscripten with the fragment's
  CARGO_TARGET_WASM32_UNKNOWN_EMSCRIPTEN_LINKER=emcc:
  target-workspace/wasm32-unknown-emscripten/debug/emhello.wasm, 4.5MB.
  (beyond #163, the exact scenario the issue measured)
- meson setup + ninja + ./build/hello prints meson-ok (CC=clang; host
  sccache wraps the default cc and fails Permission denied, environmental).
- go mod init + go build + run prints go-ok; install go writes the
  GOBIN/GOCACHE fragment. (environmental note only)
- node script ok; node $(dirname $(node -p process.execPath))/npm
  --version prints 11.19.1 (the #157 invocation); npm works.
- deno eval prints deno-ok; both doctor probe spellings rc=0 live.
- ASan binary: bash -lc prints asan-ok with ASAN_OPTIONS=[detect_leaks=0]
  (profile loads whole env.sh, beyond #155); sandhome exec prints asan-ok;
  plain sh -c dies with the LSan fatal error as documented (no init reads
  profile there).

## mechanisms, with controls

- TAR_OPTIONS=--no-same-owner live: foreign-uid (1000) tarball extracts
  rc=0; env -u TAR_OPTIONS tar -xf fails rc=2 Cannot change ownership.
  (beyond #162, exact failure reproduced without the default)
- XDG_CACHE_HOME=$SANDHOME_EXEC/cache: write+exec file runs rc=0.
  (beyond #158)
- uv/uvx: install python creates exec/bin links; install meson keeps them;
  hook serves 28 commands; fresh env -i shell resolves uv and uvx.
  (beyond #152, created AND kept)
- resume after mv exec away, via entry.sh from env -i: wanted list
  preserved incl. deno/meson/ninja; doctor FAIL toolchain_meson (payload
  was on the wiped root, honestly red, not silently green); install meson
  heals to doctor_failures=0. (beyond #153/#154)
- prebind: builds; serves HTTP over a unix socket through an inherited fd
  twice; stale path property shown via python rebind traceback.
  (beyond #160)
- report file_size_limit=1000000000; 1.2GB single write raises;
  stream | head -c works. (beyond #164)

## installed, not documented

- sandhome install emscripten: emsdk clone + SDK 3.1.73 in 47s; emcc
  --version 3.1.73; EM_CONFIG on the exec view; doctor emscripten_linker
  yes once SANDHOME_RUST_TARGETS records the target. (beyond #163)

## defects found while proving (fixed in this round)

- python fragment pinned UV_PYTHON_DOWNLOADS=never unconditionally, and the
  fragment loads inside the installer, so install --force python could never
  fetch CPython. Guarded now; override flows through; reinstall rc=0.
- backticks inside an unquoted heredoc body execute: one instance of mine
  (python fragment note) and one of the round's (rust adopted fragment
  note about rustup target add). Both reworded; a sweep clause guards all
  unquoted heredoc bodies in tools/, lib/, bin/sandhome, bootstrap.sh.
- installed python never promoted bin/uv bin/uvx, so the hook never served
  them although the description promises uv on PATH. Named now.

## suite

sh tests/run.sh: every file passed (syntax, memexec skipped, same as the
pr166 baseline). regressions-beyond-166.sh: 39 clauses, 30 of which fail
against pr166 (the rest are two-way controls).
